11.0.0 — video takes that hold the song, the screen and the beat; the Tape effect in time; exports that start on the bar

What is new since 10075. Each line is code that the Echoel Studio run builds. The take camera's
audio setting is one line with no test of its own; for the other four the rule has tests (for the
screen and the first frame, the rule, not the camera code). Nothing here is device-verified yet.
- Video takes: opening the take camera no longer changes the app's audio setup. A take records no
  sound of its own (the song is its sound), and the camera now leaves the audio alone, as the pulse
  and sky cameras already did.
- Video takes: the screen stays on while a take is filmed or saved, so Auto-Lock can no longer
  send the app away mid-take, where iOS took the camera and the take ended early. Afterwards the
  screen sleeps as usual.
- Video takes: from iOS 18.2 a take is laid where its first frame was filmed, from the time the
  camera gives that frame, not where the report of its start reached the app. On earlier iOS the
  start is timed as soon as the camera reports it. A frame time that cannot be right is not used.
- Tape effect: it plays 6 ms behind its input and now says so, so in the app the other tracks
  wait for it and a Tape track stays in time with the rest. As an Audio Unit in another app it now
  reports those 6 ms too, so a host that compensates plug-in delay can line the track up (not yet
  tried in any host); bypassed through the unit's own bypass, its dry sound stays 6 ms late. In
  Echoelmusic, a Tape set to Off leaves the chain: the track plays without the 6 ms, and the other
  tracks stop waiting for it.
- Export: an effect that plays late (Pitch Correction, 19 ms; the Multiband Clipper, 85 samples;
  another maker's plug-in that reports its delay) no longer makes an export start that late. On a
  track, a return or the master, the exported WAV (stereo or Spatial Headphone Mix) and the sound
  of Export Video begin on the song's first sample: the sound lands on the song's markers, and the
  video's sound sits on its picture. The stems and the ADM file leave the master's effects out; an
  effect on a track or a return no longer makes them late either. The same holds for a region made
  with Bake Effects. A Tape track, 6 ms late in an export before, now sits in time there too.

Known limit, unchanged since 10074: a MIDI take does not record a key held across the loop's end
as it was heard. While the record line shows "Loop Record · take n" (a loop of two bars or
longer), the note ends at the loop's end and the next take does not have it. On a one-bar loop, a
key let go after the loop came round is recorded shorter than it was held. ("take n" stands for
the number the line shows: take 1, take 2 …)
Known limit: while an effect that plays late is on a track (even a muted one), on the master, or
on a return a send feeds (Tape 6 ms, Pitch Correction 19 ms, the Multiband Clipper 85 samples),
every track waits for it.
The whole mix then plays that much behind the click, the Video Window's picture and what goes out
as MIDI, and an audio or MIDI take played along with the tracks instead of the click lands that
much late. With Tape this is new in this build.
Known limit: when the screen turns sideways (Portrait Orientation Lock off), the take camera's
preview stays a tall box and shows the picture on its side; the take itself is written upright.

Previous: 11.0.0 (10075) — the top bar rebuilt as a deck and a display on one grid; 10074 — a key
held through the loop sounds on, other makers' Audio Units in the lists, a Samples folder to hear
sounds from. Earlier: git history.

Bump this file on a commit whose "Echoel Studio" run is green to upload a build
(.github/workflows/studio-testflight.yml). Build number = 10000 + the workflow's run number.
The version is MARKETING_VERSION in EchoelStudio/project.yml.
This file is a test list for the device, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

Five things to try on the device (answer each yes or no). Overlapping video takes hide each other
in the Video Window, and Record on a stopped song starts where Stop left it (bar 1, unless a place
on the ruler was chosen): before checks 3 and 5, mute (M)
every video track that holds an earlier take, add a new video track (Add Track › Video) and use the
camera button in its row.
1. Click off (the metronome key; on a phone, More (…) › Click). A song where a synth plays for
   about a minute, and a video track. Press Play and leave the song playing. While it plays, tap
   the camera button in the video track's row, wait for the picture, tap Record, film about 20
   seconds, then tap Stop. Does the song play on without a gap, a crackle or a jump while the
   camera opens and when the take starts?
2. Auto-Lock at 30 seconds (Settings › Display & Brightness › Auto-Lock). Open the take camera,
   set the phone where it films you and tap Record before the screen dims (the screen is kept on
   from Record until the take is saved, not while the camera only shows its picture). Film two
   minutes without touching the screen, then tap Stop. Did the screen stay on, and does the sheet
   say "The take is on the song.", not "The take was cut short…"?
3. With the click on, turn to the front camera with the switch-camera key beside Record, tap
   Record, clap on the beat for a few bars, tap Stop, then Close and press Play. In the Video
   Window, does each clap's picture sit on its click? (Say which iOS version the phone runs.)
4. Two audio tracks with the same drum loop, Tape on one of them (Mixer → Effects → Add Effect →
   Tape, Wow at 0): do the two play as one, without a doubled or hollow sound?
5. Portrait Orientation Lock off. Hold the phone sideways, open the take camera, tap Record, tap
   Stop after a few seconds, then Close and press Play. In the Video Window, does the take fill the
   wide window, upright, not on its side or upside down? (The preview in the take sheet shows the
   picture on its side in this build; judge the take, not the preview.)
